  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM MDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nOverall, the upper level synoptic pattern has not changed \nsignificantly. The upper level flow across the Tri-State area \nremains mainly from the west to northwest from Saturday until around \nTuesday. During this period, the jet stream remains to the north \nnear the US/Canada border as various upper level troughs move along \nthe border. Model guidance remains in agreement showing an upper \nlevel ridge positioned near the Four Corners region. Compared to \nyesterday, the Canadian-NH, ECMWF, and GFS have aligned in showing a \nshortwave trough moving through the area on Tuesday. While models \nagree on the trough's presence in the region, its exact strength and \nsize remain somewhat uncertain. Currently, Probability of \nPrecipitation (PoPs) for Tuesday has slightly increased to 5-15%. If \nmodel trends continue, the trough could further increase Tuesday's \nprecipitation chance depending on its intensity. \n\nRegarding high temperatures for the week, values across the Tri-\nState region are expected to remain under 80 degrees for the \nmajority of the period. The warmest days are forecast to be Saturday \nand Tuesday, with highs in the low to mid 80s. On both days, these \nwarmer temperatures will primarily be focused in the northwestern \nportions of the County Warning Area (CWA). Overall, precipitation \nchances remain quite low from Saturday through Wednesday of next \nweek, with Pops generally around  5-10% each day. Tuesday continues \nto feature the \"highest\" potential, with PoPs of 5-15% dependent on \nthe upper level trough strength. \n\nWind continues to be somewhat of a concern for Monday, with \nsustained winds currently forecast at 15-25 mph and gusts of 35-45 \nmph. Guidance indicates that an 850 mb jet streak will likely set up \nover the region. Some uncertainty remains regarding the location of \nthe highest winds speeds. The LREF favors the majority of the CWA \nseeing gusts up to 35 mph, while the NBM has the highest gusts up to \n45 mph across the northern and western parts of the CWA. Both models \ndo agree on having around a 5-10% probability of gusts exceeding 50 \nmph across the Colorado counties. At this time, fire weather \nconcerns remain low, as relative humidity values are expected to \nstay above 25% each day and recent precipitation will likely leave \nsoil moisture fairly saturated.",
